<section aria-labelledby="different-title">
  <h2 id="different-title">What makes The Interdependency different</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Source authority stays with the source</h3>
    <p>Distributed work is displayed with its owning repository, path, and source identity. Publication does not transfer authority merely because related material appears on one website.</p>

    <h3>Evidence stays attached to claims</h3>
    <p>Where the publishing system can verify exact source identity, it records commits, blobs, digests, status, and fallback state. A generated page is therefore a view of evidence, not a substitute for it.</p>

    <h3>Unknown stays visible</h3>
    <p><strong>hmmm</strong> marks unresolved constraints rather than converting missing evidence into a confident answer. That boundary applies here to facts such as the LLC founding date and headquarters until an owning source verifies them.</p>

    <h3>Human and machine readers get explicit entry points</h3>
    <p>Human pages prioritize readable navigation; machine readers can use the published AI context, JSON-LD, sitemap, and <code>llms.txt</code>. These surfaces improve retrieval and grounding without claiming that the website directly trains external models.</p>

    <h3>The public reading path is static-first</h3>
    <p>Core content remains readable without JavaScript, while search, maps, and other interaction progressively enhance it. Release checks test generated routes, links, accessibility, and publication identity before deployment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

